  @Boundary @P2 @Auth @EmailField @MinBoundary
  Scenario: Login succeeds with minimum valid email length (6 characters)
    Given a user account exists with email "a@b.cc"
    When the user enters "a@b.cc" in the email field
    And the user enters the correct password
    And the user clicks Login
    Then the login should succeed
    And the user should reach the dashboard

  @Boundary @P2 @Auth @EmailField @MaxBoundary
  Scenario: Email field accepts maximum 254 characters
    Given the user navigates to the login page
    When the user attempts to enter 255 characters in the email field
    Then the field should enforce the 254-character maximum
    And input beyond the limit should be rejected or truncated

  @Boundary @P1 @Auth @PasswordField @MinBoundary
  Scenario: Login fails with password below minimum length (7 chars)
    Given the user navigates to the login page
    When the user enters "Pass12!" (7 characters) in the password field
    And the user clicks Login
    Then validation error should say "Password must be at least 8 characters"
    And the form should not be submitted

  # ==========================================
  # SECURITY SCENARIOS
  # ==========================================

  @Security @P0 @Auth @SQLInjection
  Scenario Outline: SQL injection in login fields does not authenticate
    Given the user navigates to the login page
    When the user enters "<sql_payload>" in the email field
    And the user enters "any_password" in the password field
    And the user clicks Login
    Then login should fail
    And no database error details should be exposed
    And the response should show a generic error message

    Examples:
      | sql_payload              |
      | ' OR '1'='1              |
      | admin'--                 |
      | '; DROP TABLE users;--   |
      | ' UNION SELECT 1,2,3--   |

  @Security @P0 @Auth @XSS
  Scenario Outline: XSS payloads in login fields do not execute
    Given the user navigates to the login page
    When the user enters "<xss_payload>" in the email field
    And the user clicks Login
    Then no JavaScript should execute
    And no alerts or popups should appear
    And the payload should be treated as literal text

    Examples:
      | xss_payload                          |
      | <script>alert('XSS')</script>        |
      | "><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>       |
      | <svg onload=alert(1)>                |

  @Security @P0 @Auth @Authorization @UnauthorizedAccess
  Scenario Outline: Unauthenticated access to protected pages redirects to login
    Given the user is not logged in
    When the user directly navigates to "<protected_url>"
    Then the user should be redirected to the login page
    And no authenticated content should be visible

    Examples:
      | protected_url     |
      | /dashboard        |
      | /profile          |
      | /admin            |
      | /settings         |
      | /reports          |

  @Security @P0 @Auth @Session @TokenInvalidation
  Scenario: Old session token is rejected after logout
    Given the user is logged in and the session token is captured
    When the user logs out
    And the user manually sets the old session token in the browser
    And the user navigates to a protected page
    Then the request should return 401 Unauthorized
    And no authenticated content should be accessible

  @Security @P0 @Auth @HTTPS
  Scenario: Login credentials are transmitted over HTTPS
    Given the user navigates to the login page
    When the user submits login credentials
    Then the login request should use HTTPS protocol
    And the password should NOT appear in the URL
    And the password should be sent in the POST request body

  @Security @P0 @Auth @RBAC
  Scenario: Regular user cannot access admin-only pages
    Given the user is logged in as a regular non-admin user
    When the user navigates directly to "/admin"
    Then the user should see a "403 Forbidden" or "Access Denied" message
    And no admin data should be displayed

